cepted as risk by the impl-lead. These stem from PI's API limitations, which prevent the connector from fully implementing the literal AC requirements. The fix batch hasn't materialized yet—the path doesn't exist, so no changes occurred after the epic review.\n\nThe review remains blocked on two issues that were already accepted as design risks due to PI API constraints. Since the previous review result still stands as authoritative, there's no new information that would change the assessment, and I need to determine if these risks now require formal ruling before closeout can proceed.\n\nAccording to the base prompt, I must verify evidence independently. Both findings have documented support from the codebase - the implementation acknowledges these as Epic-1 conveniences with known limitations. The Story 1 verification confirms the gates pass despite these deviations.\n\nThe core question is whether human ruling occurred. The team implementation log shows impl-lead accepted the findings as \"accepted-risk\" under standing authorization. However, my instructions require an independent check - I shouldn't simply accept previous decisions without verifying if formal ruling documentation exists. I need to locate the ruling artifacts that would formally document these accepted risks and confirm they meet the criteria for closeout.\n\nI should check for any ruling files or fix batch artifacts that would demonstrate these issues were formally addressed through proper channels rather than just internal acceptance.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7689c21-1739-4f71-b1a5-e967f006f0f9","uuid":"35366f6a-36b9-4efa-b3e2-3869d68e8c5c"}
